.TH TMUX 1 "2026-09-07" "notcoreutils" "notcoreutils Manual"
.SH NAME
tmux \- a real terminal multiplexer with sessions, windows and real-pty panes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tmux
.br
.B tmux new-session
[\fB\-s\fR \fINAME\fR]
.br
.B tmux attach
[\fB\-t\fR \fINAME\fR]
.br
.B tmux list-sessions
.br
.B tmux kill-server
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tmux
is a real client/server terminal multiplexer: sessions contain
windows, windows contain panes, and every pane is a real pty running
a real shell (or whatever
.B $SHELL
points to). The
.I server
(the first invocation, if none is already running - forked and
daemonized automatically) owns every pane and keeps every process
alive across detach/attach; every invocation after that is a thin
.I client
that connects over a Unix socket
(\fI/tmp/nctmux-<uid>/default\fR \- deliberately a different path
than real tmux's own socket, so the two can never collide), puts the
local terminal into raw mode, and both renders what the server sends
and forwards local keystrokes back.
.PP
Bare
.B tmux
(no subcommand) attaches to any already-running session, starting
both the server and a
.B main
session first if neither exists yet.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "new-session " "[-s NAME]"
Create a new session (named
.I NAME
if given, otherwise
.BR main )
and attach to it.
.TP
.BI "attach " "[-t NAME]"
Attach to session
.IR NAME ,
or to any already-running session if
.I NAME
is omitted.
.TP
.B list-sessions
Print every session's name and window count, without attaching to
anything.
.TP
.B kill-server
Stop the server and every pane it owns.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
The prefix key (default
.BR C-b ,
rebindable \- see CONFIG) must be pressed before every binding below.
.TP
.B c
Create a new window.
.TP
.B n\fR / \fBp
Switch to the next / previous window.
.TP
.B 0\fR\-\fB9
Switch directly to window N.
.TP
.B %
Split the active pane vertically (side by side).
.TP
.B "\(dq"
Split the active pane horizontally (stacked).
.TP
Arrow keys
Move to the pane in that direction.
.TP
.B x
Kill the active pane (asks
.B y
/
.B n
to confirm).
.TP
.B d
Detach \- the server and every pane keep running.
.TP
.B :
Open a command prompt. Accepts
.BR new-window ,
.BR "rename-window NAME" ,
.BR split-window\ [-h|-v] ,
.BR kill-pane ,
.BR kill-window ,
and
.BR kill-server .
This is real
.BR tmux 's
own idea of "scripting": an external shell script drives a running
server through this exact same command vocabulary (piped in as raw
keystrokes, prefix
.B :
included), not a separate embedded language.
.SH CONFIG
Read once, at server startup, from
.I $HOME/.tmux.conf
(or
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux/tmux.conf
/
.I $HOME/.config/tmux/tmux.conf
if that does not exist). One directive per line; blank lines and
.B #
comments are ignored. Supported:
.TP
.BI "set -g prefix C-" X
Rebind the prefix key to Ctrl-\fIX\fR.
.TP
.BI "set -g status-bg " N
.TQ
.BI "set -g status-fg " N
Status bar background/foreground, as an ANSI colour index (0\-255).
.PP
Every other real tmux.conf directive (including
.BR bind-key )
is recognised as "a config line" \- not a parse error \- but has no
effect yet. A real, stated scope cut, not silently mis-parsed.
.SH LIMITATIONS
No copy-mode (scrollback search/selection), no true multi-client
mirroring of one session (each attached client gets its own
independently-sized render of the same underlying panes, not a
shared view), no cross-session window linking, no hooks. The VT100/
ECMA-48 emulator backing every pane's screen is a real, but real
.I subset
\- cursor movement, erase, and SGR colour/attributes are handled;
anything else in a CSI sequence is consumed and silently ignored
rather than corrupting the display. One grid cell holds exactly one
byte \- UTF-8/wide characters pass through but are not handled with
real terminal cell-width rules.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR screen (1)
